.TH SET_INSERT 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_insert \- Insert an element into a set

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_insert(set_t* set, const void* val, const size_t size);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_insert\fR function inserts an element into a set. It takes three arguments: the set, a pointer to the element, and the size of the element. The function returns 1 if the element is successfully inserted, 2 if the element already exists in the set, and -1 if an error occurs.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set into which to insert the element.
.TP
.IB val
A pointer to the element to insert.
.TP
.IB size
The size of the element.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the element is successfully inserted, 2 if the element already exists in the set, and -1 if an error occurs.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_insert\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    char* element = "apple";

    set_init(&set);
    if (set_insert(&set, element, strlen(element)) == 1) {
        printf("Element inserted successfully\n");
    } else if (set_insert(&set, element, strlen(element)) == 2) {
        printf("Element already exists in set\n");
    } else {
        printf("Error inserting element\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef